WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:08.510
AMIT SINGHAL: When I was young,
I was fascinated by planes

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:11.070
and wanted to be a pilot.

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:13.330
I first got a chance
to fly on a plane

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:16.180
when I was in middle
school, and what I remember

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:18.446
is how smooth the ride was.

00:00:18.446 --> 00:00:21.460
I did not even feel
that we were moving.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:25.580
When I grew up, I had a chance
to look inside the cockpit

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:28.570
and I saw how complex it was.

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:30.760
But still, the
experience of flying

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:34.300
was made smooth and
easy for the passengers.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:38.030
And that is how Google
Cloud Platform operates.

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:41.410
It has complexity
inside, but for the user,

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:43.990
it is simple and easy to use.

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:47.830
And it is reliable
like planes too.

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:54.276
Hi everyone, I'm Amit Singhal,
Technical Lead at Google.

00:00:54.276 --> 00:00:56.280
Today, along with
my colleagues, we'll

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:00.540
talk about how Google
software defined approach,

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.720
behind its infrastructure
and products,

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:06.140
make it a resilient, scalable
and high performance.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:09.760
We'll deep dive into
Andromeda virtual networking

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:13.290
and also talk about front
end systems that route

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:15.330
all incoming traffic.

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:16.846
In the end, we'll
talk about some

00:01:16.846 --> 00:01:17.970
of the networking products.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:25.290
Google Cloud Platform at high
level comprises the following.

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:29.220
Compute, which has Compute
Engine, App Engine, Container

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:32.220
Engine, and Cloud Functions.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:34.830
Then we have storage,
which has several products

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:40.530
like Cloud SQL, BigTable, Cloud
Data Store, Spanner, et cetera.

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:44.190
Networking, with infrastructure
for virtualization and load

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:47.700
balancers, and several
products like cloud VPN,

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:50.890
routers, firewalls, et cetera.

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:53.620
Analytics, with
products like BigQuery,

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:58.770
Dataflow, Dataproc, machine
learning, et cetera.

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:01.380
One key advantage Google
Cloud customers get

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:04.100
is reliability and
scale of running

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:07.190
on same shared
infrastructure that powers

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:11.880
all Google services, like
search, ads, maps, Gmail, et

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:16.010
cetera, built with years of
experience and state of the art

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:18.980
technology.

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:21.470
Google has been a pioneer
in large scale distributed

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:24.470
systems for more than a decade.

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:26.990
Many of Google's
innovations have completely

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:29.180
changed the standard
computing paradigms

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:32.120
and spawned new industries.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:35.060
MagProduce started
big data revolution,

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:38.510
Cloud DataFlow is
built over that.

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:43.370
BigTable was one of the first
NoSQL database implementations,

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:46.610
and Cloud BigTable
is built over that.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:49.410
Then we have Cloud
GCS over Colossus.

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:52.400
Cloud BigQuery is
built over Dremel.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.820
Spanner is a wide area
consistent data base service

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:59.090
that allows you to run
transactions across the globe,

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:03.030
and Cloud Spanner is
provided over that.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:05.810
All of these services are
provided through gRCP APIs.

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:11.710
We use software defined
approach across all our tickets

00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:15.040
of Cloud, and they
share similar concepts.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:17.170
We would love to talk
about all the areas,

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:18.790
but in the interest
of time, we'll

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:20.890
focus more on the
networking in this talk.

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:27.000
Google is constantly
innovating in networking space.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:30.720
This is a timeline of
the major milestones.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:33.150
We have taken software
defined approach early on,

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:35.790
in almost everything we do.

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:38.100
Maglev was a software
load balancer

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:42.920
that routes all incoming
traffic to collect back ends.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.950
B4 is our WAN network
built using SDN principles.

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:52.850
Jupiter is our software
defined DataCenter networking,

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:56.750
and Andromeda is our software
defined network virtualization

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:59.770
technology.

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:02.650
Why Google Cloud networking?

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:06.130
It provides high throughput,
up to 16 gigabits per second

00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:10.900
and under 100 microsecond
latency between VMs.

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:15.070
You can spin up thousands
of VMs within a few minutes,

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:17.740
and we provide hitless
upgrades and feature rollouts

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:21.160
without impacting customer
workloads with our VM migration

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:23.470
technology.

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:25.540
One of the key advantages
of our networks

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:28.480
are that they're global,
which allows customers

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:33.350
to have VMs in different regions
communicate over private IPs.

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:36.610
And we provide network functions
as a service, like load

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:41.810
balancing, routing,
security, CDN, et cetera.

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:43.840
We talked about why,
and now let's look

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:46.350
at what is Google
Cloud networking.

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:48.310
Like I said, we at
Google use software

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:51.880
defined networking
as DN in everything

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:57.130
we do for networking, but Google
Cloud networking is not just

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:58.600
that.

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:02.560
First of all, it is built
upon our state of the art WAN

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:06.580
connecting our state of
the art data centers.

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:12.100
It is network virtualization and
isolation using SDN principles.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:15.150
It has to scale to
support thousands of VMs

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:18.520
for thousands of
customers and provide

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:22.420
customizable functionality
through APIs.

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:24.940
All this together make
Google Cloud network.

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:30.990
So software defined
network virtualization.

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:34.720
What are the benefits
for the user?

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:37.170
It provides faster
provisioning times

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:41.560
with centralized controller,
faster roll out of new features

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:45.430
without depending on new
hardware, high availability

00:05:45.430 --> 00:05:49.900
using hierarchical
control plane.

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:52.560
In next few slides,
we'll talk about

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:54.210
how virtual networks
are different

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:57.940
from physical networks, and what
are the unique challenges that

00:05:57.940 --> 00:05:59.790
creates.

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:02.640
Overlay virtual networks
over physical network,

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:05.460
and we'll also talk about
our centralized hierarchical

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:06.100
control plane.

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:12.780
Some high level background on
how physical networks look.

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:14.350
You have racks of servers.

00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:18.140
We call them hosts here,
shown in green boxes.

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:21.340
They connect to top of
the rack switch TORs.

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:24.330
TORs connect to each other
through one or more routers

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:26.530
in the middle.

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:28.050
Important property
of these networks

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:30.940
is address aggregation
and locality.

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:34.240
That limits the radius of
layer to broadcast domain,

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:36.300
and that is also
used by distributor

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:39.160
routing protocols and
switches to learn and make

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:42.550
forwarding decisions.

00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:47.740
With Cloud, physical data center
networks become multi-tenant.

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:49.810
You can imagine there
is a logical switch

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:52.200
for virtual network.

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:56.850
Now one way to support this is
by extending the whole stack

00:06:56.850 --> 00:07:01.620
to become aware of multitenancy
and use tenant key--

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:04.920
in this case we call it VNet
here for virtual network--

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:07.830
in making all
forwarding decisions.

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:11.340
Now this need changes to
physical switches and routers,

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:13.650
and also make routing
protocols for getting

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:16.290
this new information.

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:19.350
But even if we do that, what
makes this approach difficult

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:22.290
is that as customers
spin out new VMs,

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:25.080
they can be scheduled anywhere
on the physical network,

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:27.480
depending on the availability.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.080
And there is no location
based address aggregation.

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:36.850
Network virtualization
takes a different approach.

00:07:36.850 --> 00:07:40.600
Instead of relying on dynamic
discovery based protocols,

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:44.730
it creates a logical
overlay for each customer,

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:46.930
hiding details of
the physical network.

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:55.080
Using encapsulation and host
port information for each VM,

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:58.760
point to point flows
are created between VMs.

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:01.007
Now centralized controller
is key to implementing

00:08:01.007 --> 00:08:01.590
such approach.

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:07.610
Now let's look at workflow
for VM creation and network

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:09.630
provisioning.

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:12.290
In this case, owner
of the orange network

00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:16.130
wants to add one more VM.

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:19.930
He sends the request to
configuration manager,

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:22.560
which cause computer
control plane to start

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:27.350
a VM on some host with
available capacity.

00:08:27.350 --> 00:08:30.350
That information is given to
the Configuration Manager, which

00:08:30.350 --> 00:08:34.370
calls the network control plane
with the host port information

00:08:34.370 --> 00:08:37.490
of the newly created VM.

00:08:37.490 --> 00:08:40.100
It then sends a message
to all hosts which

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:44.110
have VM from this network,
and point to point flows

00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:46.950
are created from this new
VM to the existing VMs.

00:08:50.470 --> 00:08:53.420
In this slide, I'd
like to emphasize

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:56.740
the hierarchical implementation
of our configuration

00:08:56.740 --> 00:08:58.710
and control plane.

00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:02.530
Configuration plane
serves all user requests

00:09:02.530 --> 00:09:05.570
by calling appropriate
backend modules.

00:09:05.570 --> 00:09:10.490
It is divided into three levels,
global, regional, and zonal.

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:14.260
Even backend control plane
is divided into three levels,

00:09:14.260 --> 00:09:16.850
global, regional, and zonal.

00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:19.640
User requests are routed
to various backend jobs

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:21.620
at different hierarchies.

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:25.130
Now the goal is to
keep most configuration

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:27.590
and provisioning at
zonal level to limit

00:09:27.590 --> 00:09:31.685
the blast radius of any failure,
and then regional, and only

00:09:31.685 --> 00:09:34.435
a few services to be
global when necessary.

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:40.280
We talked about
configuration, now

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:43.100
let's look at control
and data plane.

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:47.130
Fabric Manager gets all requests
for network provisioning.

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:51.650
Again, tasks are divided at
regional and zonal level.

00:09:51.650 --> 00:09:54.700
Their VM controllers,
which manages all the VMs

00:09:54.700 --> 00:10:00.110
inside customer network and
those VM controllers are zonal,

00:10:00.110 --> 00:10:03.830
it creates flows between all the
endpoints on a virtual network,

00:10:03.830 --> 00:10:06.740
so these end points
can communicate.

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:11.460
Our next speaker will talk in
more detail about this part.

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:13.670
There are Cloud
front-end tasks that

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:17.160
provide connectivity to
Google services for VMs

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:20.350
through internal network,
without having to travel

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:23.870
through public network.

00:10:23.870 --> 00:10:26.170
End points inside
virtual network

00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:30.410
can send traffic to internet
through Google edge routers.

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:33.960
And finally, all
incoming traffic

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:36.530
first hits network load
balancer and appropriately

00:10:36.530 --> 00:10:40.870
routed to correct endpoint
in either customer network

00:10:40.870 --> 00:10:42.770
or Google front-end
tasks for Google.

00:10:42.770 --> 00:10:44.412
Services.

00:10:44.412 --> 00:10:48.280
And now, I'll hand over to my
colleague and next speaker,

00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:51.347
Krishnan to talk more about
Andromeda virtual networking.

00:10:57.420 --> 00:10:58.870
SRINIVAS KRISHNAN Thanks Amit.

00:10:58.870 --> 00:11:01.802
In this portion of the talk,
I will be covering details

00:11:01.802 --> 00:11:03.760
on the network, virtualization
of the structure

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:05.620
that powers GCP.

00:11:05.620 --> 00:11:07.940
This is a great opportunity
for us, especially me,

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:12.000
to represent the work of an
amazing team here at Google.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:13.920
So network
virtualization for GCP

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:16.290
internally is
known as Andromeda.

00:11:16.290 --> 00:11:20.030
Andromeda powers the virtual
networking infrastructure

00:11:20.030 --> 00:11:22.880
and a lot of our network
product offerings.

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:25.460
Amin Vahdat, Google Fellow,
has presented our Andromeda

00:11:25.460 --> 00:11:28.580
at ONF earlier,
and today we will

00:11:28.580 --> 00:11:31.400
be uncovering the nuts and bolts
of how Andromeda's actually

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:34.400
built, especially its
core features that

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:38.980
allows us to build scalable
and high performance systems.

00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:42.670
So what you see behind
me is GCP's network.

00:11:42.670 --> 00:11:45.390
It is one of the largest
networks among cloud providers.

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:49.480
It is globally spread out,
there are six regions, 18 zones,

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:53.050
and we will be announcing
12 more regions in 2017.

00:11:53.050 --> 00:11:55.390
Network virtualization
is to build,

00:11:55.390 --> 00:11:57.370
to make it easy for
you, the customer,

00:11:57.370 --> 00:12:01.260
to leverage this
amazing network.

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:03.580
This talk is in two halves.

00:12:03.580 --> 00:12:06.580
First I'll be doing a deep
dive into our control plane.

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:09.180
First I'll be talking about
how we provide isolation

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:11.100
across virtual networks.

00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:14.410
In this example, there
are two virtual networks

00:12:14.410 --> 00:12:16.780
which share the same
physical infrastructure.

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:19.600
I will be giving details on how
network virtualization allows

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:22.516
these networks to have
their own tenant space.

00:12:22.516 --> 00:12:26.280
I will also be talking about
how we can scale up on demand.

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:28.720
GCP, we have done
experiments where

00:12:28.720 --> 00:12:34.600
we can show 100k VMs can be
spun up in 184 milliseconds.

00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:36.160
I shall also be
going into details

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:38.830
about how we achieve
high availability targets

00:12:38.830 --> 00:12:43.030
and do three nines in a single
zone, four nines in a region,

00:12:43.030 --> 00:12:45.070
and five nines globally.

00:12:45.070 --> 00:12:49.060
Next, I will also be going into
details about our unique data

00:12:49.060 --> 00:12:49.960
plane.

00:12:49.960 --> 00:12:52.030
Our data plane has
core network functions

00:12:52.030 --> 00:12:55.230
that bake directly into it,
for example load balancing.

00:12:55.230 --> 00:12:58.510
We're also constantly evolving,
and we are building new network

00:12:58.510 --> 00:13:00.580
functions into our data plane.

00:13:00.580 --> 00:13:03.310
I'll be talking into details
and how we achieve that

00:13:03.310 --> 00:13:05.350
by using live
migration, and also

00:13:05.350 --> 00:13:09.160
what we call hitless
dataplane upgrades.

00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:11.320
So let's dive right in.

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:14.240
As Amit mentioned, our control
plane is built as a hierarchy.

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:16.980
Sits at the very top, where
we call cluster manager.

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:19.870
And cluster manager
performs many tasks.

00:13:19.870 --> 00:13:21.610
It is responsible for
configuring things

00:13:21.610 --> 00:13:27.040
like storage, compute, and
in our case, networking.

00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:29.170
Below that sits
the fabric manager,

00:13:29.170 --> 00:13:30.805
and you can see a
theme going on here.

00:13:30.805 --> 00:13:33.340
It's again split up into
regional and zonal control

00:13:33.340 --> 00:13:34.720
planes.

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:37.960
Andromeda comes below it,
which is VM controllers,

00:13:37.960 --> 00:13:40.780
and what the fabric manager,
the VM controller's job,

00:13:40.780 --> 00:13:43.420
is to expose a high
level API, which

00:13:43.420 --> 00:13:47.170
is used to configure the
virtual networks and also

00:13:47.170 --> 00:13:49.890
different network functions.

00:13:49.890 --> 00:13:53.520
Right below it sits the OFE,
which is the open flow layer.

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:56.550
It takes our API abstractions
and then converts them

00:13:56.550 --> 00:13:58.440
into OpenFlow
programming constructs

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:00.892
that we can use to
program a switching layer.

00:14:00.892 --> 00:14:03.360
The switching layer shown
here is built directly

00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:05.010
into each VM host.

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:08.280
It has a programmable switch,
which is based on open vswitch,

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:09.780
and it has a core
data plane, which

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:11.561
is responsible for
packet processing.

00:14:14.790 --> 00:14:17.270
So now let's take a deeper look
on how you actually achieve

00:14:17.270 --> 00:14:19.450
the scale of a control plane.

00:14:19.450 --> 00:14:22.510
I've zoomed in here, a zonal
view of our control plane

00:14:22.510 --> 00:14:24.450
and a single host
that's managing.

00:14:24.450 --> 00:14:28.010
A zone shares the same
hardware failure domain

00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:30.480
and also has the same
network connectivity.

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:32.990
To guard against
machine failures,

00:14:32.990 --> 00:14:34.940
the VM controllers
are replicated.

00:14:34.940 --> 00:14:38.210
It has one singly master,
[INAUDIBLE] selected master

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:41.300
and two standbys to take
over at any given time.

00:14:41.300 --> 00:14:44.520
We also sharded the
OFEs, and a single OFE

00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:48.590
maintains shards,
multiple VM host shards.

00:14:48.590 --> 00:14:52.080
And we take this sharding
concept one step further.

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:53.780
We replicate our
entire control plane

00:14:53.780 --> 00:14:56.260
so that each VMC also
manages other racks

00:14:56.260 --> 00:14:57.860
on other set of hosts.

00:14:57.860 --> 00:15:00.020
This allows us to
smear processing

00:15:00.020 --> 00:15:02.270
across these different replicas.

00:15:02.270 --> 00:15:05.750
Finally this also allows
for easy horizontal scaling.

00:15:05.750 --> 00:15:07.952
If you want to add new
capacity to our zones,

00:15:07.952 --> 00:15:09.910
let's say we bring up a
whole new set of racks,

00:15:09.910 --> 00:15:13.500
all we need to do is spin up
a new replica of our control

00:15:13.500 --> 00:15:16.730
plane while you have a new
capacity can be announced.

00:15:16.730 --> 00:15:20.120
It is as simple as that.

00:15:20.120 --> 00:15:23.830
So as GCP has grown and our
offerings have expanded,

00:15:23.830 --> 00:15:26.840
the network programming has
become more and more complex.

00:15:26.840 --> 00:15:28.620
We started off with
a very simple model.

00:15:28.620 --> 00:15:31.070
It's a pre-programmed model,
it looks something like this.

00:15:31.070 --> 00:15:32.830
It's a full mesh.

00:15:32.830 --> 00:15:34.930
It is very simple,
consistent, and predictable

00:15:34.930 --> 00:15:37.720
to think about in latency,
but it has one problem.

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:40.200
It has quadratic
scaling in terms of VMs.

00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:42.820
So any time, for a network, if
you're going to add a new VM,

00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:44.420
you've got to do this full mesh.

00:15:44.420 --> 00:15:46.970
That means you've got to go
ahead and program OpenFlow

00:15:46.970 --> 00:15:49.670
rules in every single VM host.

00:15:49.670 --> 00:15:51.100
We can do better than that.

00:15:51.100 --> 00:15:52.194
So we started looking.

00:15:52.194 --> 00:15:54.610
The first thing we evaluated
were the traditional OpenFlow

00:15:54.610 --> 00:15:58.000
model, where the control plane
waits for the first packet,

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:00.790
and based on the packet,
we install our flows.

00:16:00.790 --> 00:16:03.450
Now this gets much better
than the pre-programmed model,

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:05.110
but it has
unpredictable latency,

00:16:05.110 --> 00:16:06.520
and you can imagine why.

00:16:06.520 --> 00:16:09.650
It is dependent on the
control plane capacity.

00:16:09.650 --> 00:16:11.730
It also exposes
the control plane

00:16:11.730 --> 00:16:16.340
to packet floods from the hosts.

00:16:16.340 --> 00:16:20.477
So what we did is we combined
the best features of all

00:16:20.477 --> 00:16:22.060
these different
things, and we came up

00:16:22.060 --> 00:16:24.300
with the model, what
we call Hoverboards.

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:26.020
Hoverboards are a
software gateway,

00:16:26.020 --> 00:16:29.140
and we you can think of this
as a default router network.

00:16:29.140 --> 00:16:31.090
We don't need to
pre-program our flows.

00:16:31.090 --> 00:16:33.235
As soon as the VMs
come up in a network,

00:16:33.235 --> 00:16:35.421
the packets start
egressing and ingressing

00:16:35.421 --> 00:16:37.420
through the software
gateway, so there is really

00:16:37.420 --> 00:16:39.500
no warm up time.

00:16:39.500 --> 00:16:41.140
Small networks remain as before.

00:16:41.140 --> 00:16:43.000
We borrowed this from
the pre-program model,

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:48.340
as I mentioned before, it is
simple and it's consistent.

00:16:48.340 --> 00:16:51.750
For this Hoverboards, as the
flows that are going through it

00:16:51.750 --> 00:16:53.920
grow larger, we also
added the ability

00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:55.730
to offload them directly
back to the host.

00:16:55.730 --> 00:16:58.630
And this allows our
software gateways to scale,

00:16:58.630 --> 00:17:03.220
as well as giving predictable
latency and throughput.

00:17:03.220 --> 00:17:05.640
From this, I'm going to move
onto the data plane now.

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:07.950
So we've been working
on this for a while.

00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:10.720
The first step
was Andromeda 1.0,

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:14.450
but our data plane was in the
kernel, and it had openvswitch.

00:17:14.450 --> 00:17:17.310
To that, we added
offloads for performance

00:17:17.310 --> 00:17:18.900
and we introduced
live migration.

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:21.680
This was Andromeda 1.5.

00:17:21.680 --> 00:17:24.335
Next is what we have the
current version, which is 2.0.

00:17:24.335 --> 00:17:26.970
It is a large evolution, where
we removed the entire data

00:17:26.970 --> 00:17:29.610
plane from the kernel
to use the space.

00:17:29.610 --> 00:17:32.010
With it came a huge
boost in performance

00:17:32.010 --> 00:17:35.100
and also a large increase
in release velocity.

00:17:35.100 --> 00:17:38.010
And I'll be talking about
how we achieved that.

00:17:38.010 --> 00:17:40.620
In the next few iterations,
we pushing hard and hypervisor

00:17:40.620 --> 00:17:41.235
bypass.

00:17:41.235 --> 00:17:43.110
The first version that
we're working on right

00:17:43.110 --> 00:17:46.530
now is 2.1, which
allows the guest OS

00:17:46.530 --> 00:17:49.020
to bypass the hypervisor
and talk directly

00:17:49.020 --> 00:17:50.820
to a userspace NIC.

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:53.420
In the future, we'll
have full bypass,

00:17:53.420 --> 00:17:55.200
where the guest OS
will be directly

00:17:55.200 --> 00:17:57.750
talking to hardware NIC, giving
you full Hypervisor bypass.

00:18:00.792 --> 00:18:02.750
So one of the goals when
we built this new data

00:18:02.750 --> 00:18:05.630
plane Andromeda 2.0 was
to enable native hardware

00:18:05.630 --> 00:18:07.570
performance in software.

00:18:07.570 --> 00:18:09.350
So the way we did
that is we introduced

00:18:09.350 --> 00:18:11.870
what we call a fast path.

00:18:11.870 --> 00:18:14.980
And a fast path has a single
CPU that's busy polling

00:18:14.980 --> 00:18:17.250
and it's optimized
for performance.

00:18:17.250 --> 00:18:20.120
It can sustain up to two
million packets per second.

00:18:20.120 --> 00:18:23.660
CPU intensive operations,
we moved off the fast path

00:18:23.660 --> 00:18:25.860
into what we call coprocessors.

00:18:25.860 --> 00:18:28.310
Coprocessors have
this unique ability

00:18:28.310 --> 00:18:31.920
to scale with the technology
we call CPU attribution.

00:18:31.920 --> 00:18:33.650
And finally, all of
this is controlled

00:18:33.650 --> 00:18:38.250
by the on host control
plane using openvswitch.

00:18:38.250 --> 00:18:39.956
So you might have a
question as to why

00:18:39.956 --> 00:18:41.330
did we build this
new user space,

00:18:41.330 --> 00:18:44.460
and I shall mention three
distinct advantages.

00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:46.460
The first one is security.

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:50.870
Our data plane runs without
any root privileges.

00:18:50.870 --> 00:18:53.690
Unlike a monolithic
kernel, it also

00:18:53.690 --> 00:18:56.150
has a very small attack
surface because it's

00:18:56.150 --> 00:18:59.090
built for one purpose, so we
can do continuous fuzz testing

00:18:59.090 --> 00:19:00.140
on it.

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:03.210
It also comes built
with ASLR and has stack

00:19:03.210 --> 00:19:06.093
protected memory to prevent
against memory corruption.

00:19:09.480 --> 00:19:11.467
It also gives us
improved robustness.

00:19:11.467 --> 00:19:14.050
So if you think of this, if you
have a kernel networking stack

00:19:14.050 --> 00:19:17.100
bug, it'll take down
your entire machine.

00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:20.280
In this case, it's
a userspace process,

00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:22.500
so it'll just come right up.

00:19:22.500 --> 00:19:25.440
Finally, one of the biggest
advantages we have is rapid

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:26.590
releases.

00:19:26.590 --> 00:19:28.710
We can upgrade the
entire data plane

00:19:28.710 --> 00:19:32.790
underneath without having to
either live migrate the VMs

00:19:32.790 --> 00:19:34.444
or do a machine grain.

00:19:37.170 --> 00:19:39.600
It's not just performance
that we optimize for.

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:42.300
You also want a fully
programmable dataplane.

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:45.490
What I've shown here is a
dataplane with a flow table.

00:19:45.490 --> 00:19:47.430
Now the flow table
can take a five table,

00:19:47.430 --> 00:19:50.550
or a three table key match and
has extremely extensible packet

00:19:50.550 --> 00:19:51.880
actions.

00:19:51.880 --> 00:19:54.270
The VM controller has
full control over this.

00:19:54.270 --> 00:19:56.880
It can program it,
and in this case,

00:19:56.880 --> 00:20:00.190
it is programming you through
openflow, through openvswitch

00:20:00.190 --> 00:20:05.780
by just programming, let's say
an encap and a decap action.

00:20:05.780 --> 00:20:08.450
We also have extended
the openvswitch,

00:20:08.450 --> 00:20:10.325
so we have Google
extension frameworks

00:20:10.325 --> 00:20:12.950
that allows us to provide state
flow functions like sticky load

00:20:12.950 --> 00:20:14.300
balancing.

00:20:14.300 --> 00:20:16.640
You can do things like
stats and filtering

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:21.050
and policy enforcement directly
inside the openvswitch.

00:20:21.050 --> 00:20:23.240
We also have extended
the coprocessor API,

00:20:23.240 --> 00:20:25.430
it's extremely extensible.

00:20:25.430 --> 00:20:28.010
Anything that requires
inline packet processing,

00:20:28.010 --> 00:20:30.700
for example traffic
shaping, DoS and abuse,

00:20:30.700 --> 00:20:32.700
the built in [INAUDIBLE]
inside the coprocessor,

00:20:32.700 --> 00:20:34.880
and has API exposed as well.

00:20:34.880 --> 00:20:37.310
And any new features
we're actively developing

00:20:37.310 --> 00:20:41.560
are getting implemented in
this coprocessor framework.

00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:44.206
So next, I want to
talk about performance.

00:20:44.206 --> 00:20:45.580
So what is the
advantage that you

00:20:45.580 --> 00:20:49.040
get when we move from
Andromeda 1.0 point onwards?

00:20:49.040 --> 00:20:50.590
So first up, throughput.

00:20:50.590 --> 00:20:54.140
We improved throughput by
over 5x from Andromeda 1.0

00:20:54.140 --> 00:20:55.950
to Andromeda 2.1.

00:20:55.950 --> 00:20:58.090
So currently without
throttling, you

00:20:58.090 --> 00:21:01.120
can get 25 gbps in
a 40 G NIC setup.

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:05.160
Just note though, customers are
throttled at 60 gbps overall.

00:21:05.160 --> 00:21:06.910
The system provides
consistent performance

00:21:06.910 --> 00:21:10.120
across different
hardware platforms.

00:21:10.120 --> 00:21:11.950
We also improved latency.

00:21:11.950 --> 00:21:14.940
TCP roundtrip
latency reduced by 7x

00:21:14.940 --> 00:21:19.770
from when we move from
Andromeda 1.0 to Andromeda 2.1.

00:21:19.770 --> 00:21:22.195
Finally, CP efficiency.

00:21:22.195 --> 00:21:25.080
We measure CP efficiency
as cycles per byte,

00:21:25.080 --> 00:21:28.450
and this combines the
receive side guest,

00:21:28.450 --> 00:21:31.106
the receive side host,
even the central side host

00:21:31.106 --> 00:21:35.190
and the center side VM, and we
improved it by a factor of 8

00:21:35.190 --> 00:21:38.300
giving more CPU to the guest
to use for other functions.

00:21:38.300 --> 00:21:41.229
And with that, we wrap up the
Andromeda section of this,

00:21:41.229 --> 00:21:43.770
and I'll be handing you over to
my colleague Assar Westerlund

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:45.699
to discuss how we take
the software approach

00:21:45.699 --> 00:21:46.740
all the way to the front.

00:21:51.215 --> 00:21:52.590
ASSAR WESTERLUND:
Thank you, Sri.

00:21:52.590 --> 00:21:57.120
I will be talking about how
this cloud networking fits

00:21:57.120 --> 00:21:59.460
into the internet at large.

00:21:59.460 --> 00:22:01.770
So where do we connect
to the internet?

00:22:01.770 --> 00:22:05.320
This is a map behind me
here of where we have POPs,

00:22:05.320 --> 00:22:08.640
or points of presence, where
we pair with other networks.

00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:12.420
Our global network expands to
the world and connected data

00:22:12.420 --> 00:22:13.260
centers.

00:22:13.260 --> 00:22:16.350
But to make sure
it's truly useful,

00:22:16.350 --> 00:22:19.800
we want to connect it to
ISPs and be closer to users.

00:22:19.800 --> 00:22:25.380
We are pairing with ISPs at over
100 interconnection facilities

00:22:25.380 --> 00:22:29.040
and at about 90 internet
exchange points.

00:22:29.040 --> 00:22:31.590
About 80 of those are used
for the load balancing

00:22:31.590 --> 00:22:35.130
and CDN features that
I will talk about.

00:22:35.130 --> 00:22:37.170
As an indication of
the distances here

00:22:37.170 --> 00:22:40.380
and being a network folks, we
measure at the milliseconds,

00:22:40.380 --> 00:22:42.480
Cedexis is just the
third party affiliation

00:22:42.480 --> 00:22:45.090
of our CDN for the
cloud offering,

00:22:45.090 --> 00:22:47.760
and their numbers are
that the media latency

00:22:47.760 --> 00:22:49.710
for fetching an
object from the CDN

00:22:49.710 --> 00:22:52.610
is 42 milliseconds
across the world.

00:22:52.610 --> 00:22:56.190
And then 90 percentile
of that is 148, so not

00:22:56.190 --> 00:22:57.790
that much higher as it is.

00:22:57.790 --> 00:23:01.770
And like lots of other things,
that we care about the tail

00:23:01.770 --> 00:23:04.150
as well.

00:23:04.150 --> 00:23:05.870
So what does this look like.

00:23:05.870 --> 00:23:07.320
This is an example
here behind me

00:23:07.320 --> 00:23:10.745
of how you would
configure global load

00:23:10.745 --> 00:23:14.100
balancing into the cloud.

00:23:14.100 --> 00:23:17.200
One feature we have is that
we have a global IP address.

00:23:17.200 --> 00:23:19.230
So when you configure
a load balancer,

00:23:19.230 --> 00:23:23.220
you get one global IP address
that is valid for all users,

00:23:23.220 --> 00:23:27.324
and more importantly it gets
good service to all the users.

00:23:27.324 --> 00:23:28.740
So this means you
don't have to do

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:31.110
any DNS based load balancing.

00:23:31.110 --> 00:23:32.940
The only thing you
need to do is put

00:23:32.940 --> 00:23:36.600
in a record in your DNS
server of the choice

00:23:36.600 --> 00:23:38.400
with that address.

00:23:38.400 --> 00:23:41.260
And as you can see
on the picture here,

00:23:41.260 --> 00:23:45.877
your users will get redirected--
will get content from as nearby

00:23:45.877 --> 00:23:46.710
as we can make them.

00:23:46.710 --> 00:23:52.230
So the Des Moines users get
connected to US central,

00:23:52.230 --> 00:23:56.310
and the Mumbai users get
connected to an Asia region,

00:23:56.310 --> 00:23:59.400
regardless of anything
you have to do.

00:23:59.400 --> 00:24:01.890
The only thing you have
to do is to make sure

00:24:01.890 --> 00:24:03.990
that you use this IP address.

00:24:03.990 --> 00:24:08.550
And this is, again,
like my co-presenters

00:24:08.550 --> 00:24:11.580
talked about before, built
on the existing shared

00:24:11.580 --> 00:24:14.520
infrastructure that we at Google
use for all the Google services

00:24:14.520 --> 00:24:17.350
that you are used to.

00:24:17.350 --> 00:24:20.740
And nearby the user, we
allow you to have a CDN,

00:24:20.740 --> 00:24:22.930
it's a one checkbox
option if you

00:24:22.930 --> 00:24:25.750
want to have CDN with your
load balancing offering.

00:24:25.750 --> 00:24:30.160
We can do different routing on
a per request level on each URL,

00:24:30.160 --> 00:24:32.510
and I will talk more about that.

00:24:32.510 --> 00:24:34.810
And the latency matters for us.

00:24:34.810 --> 00:24:38.420
Latency matters for
users of web services.

00:24:38.420 --> 00:24:41.410
We have known this with search,
for example, for a long time.

00:24:41.410 --> 00:24:43.114
Any of those matter
for micro services,

00:24:43.114 --> 00:24:44.530
if you build your
infrastructures,

00:24:44.530 --> 00:24:46.480
that you are calling
different things,

00:24:46.480 --> 00:24:49.630
being able to get back
soon, is important.

00:24:49.630 --> 00:24:53.310
So that is something
that we care about.

00:24:53.310 --> 00:24:57.980
So this is an overall picture
of what looks like at the edge.

00:24:57.980 --> 00:25:00.470
Your phone in the ISPs
network on the left

00:25:00.470 --> 00:25:04.950
here is connecting to
your global IP address.

00:25:04.950 --> 00:25:08.450
We pair with this ISP, either
directly or indirectly,

00:25:08.450 --> 00:25:11.580
at some point using the
border gateway protocol,

00:25:11.580 --> 00:25:15.540
and in the edge POP, we have
a little bit of infrastructure

00:25:15.540 --> 00:25:19.290
so that we can serve
cash content from the CDN

00:25:19.290 --> 00:25:22.440
and we can terminate a TCP
connection from the user there,

00:25:22.440 --> 00:25:25.300
which brings a performance
advantage in that it's

00:25:25.300 --> 00:25:28.020
a shorter connection,
retransmissions are much faster

00:25:28.020 --> 00:25:30.270
and you get better
performance over it.

00:25:30.270 --> 00:25:33.324
And then over on the
right, further away

00:25:33.324 --> 00:25:34.740
from the user in
the general case,

00:25:34.740 --> 00:25:39.260
you have your data center, where
you run the actual VM jobs.

00:25:39.260 --> 00:25:42.100
So I'll go into more
detail on these steps.

00:25:42.100 --> 00:25:45.490
So the advantage for
doing this in software,

00:25:45.490 --> 00:25:47.650
like you heard
from Amir and Sri,

00:25:47.650 --> 00:25:50.110
is that we are very
used to building systems

00:25:50.110 --> 00:25:51.940
that scale out.

00:25:51.940 --> 00:25:54.370
We build-- we scale up
a number of components.

00:25:54.370 --> 00:25:56.730
We can have a larger
amount of traffic,

00:25:56.730 --> 00:25:58.890
larger amount of volume.

00:25:58.890 --> 00:26:01.600
It scales with new
hardware, gets better,

00:26:01.600 --> 00:26:04.930
and since the system is built
to deal with failures, one

00:26:04.930 --> 00:26:08.830
of those not being there,
we just work around that

00:26:08.830 --> 00:26:10.810
and we keep going.

00:26:10.810 --> 00:26:13.137
Also, this is a
similar mechanism

00:26:13.137 --> 00:26:14.470
that we use for pushing updates.

00:26:14.470 --> 00:26:16.510
We can take one
component off, we

00:26:16.510 --> 00:26:19.870
can push a new version of binary
to it, bring it back up again,

00:26:19.870 --> 00:26:22.120
it gets part of the mesh,
it gets running again.

00:26:22.120 --> 00:26:24.730
And we can do rolling
upgrades that way,

00:26:24.730 --> 00:26:26.770
without anybody noticing
that anything was down.

00:26:26.770 --> 00:26:30.250
So at some point, we
start with version x,

00:26:30.250 --> 00:26:32.140
and then slowly,
assuming everything

00:26:32.140 --> 00:26:33.990
goes well, we end
up with a version x

00:26:33.990 --> 00:26:36.340
plus one with the new features.

00:26:36.340 --> 00:26:38.920
And if something
doesn't go well,

00:26:38.920 --> 00:26:41.731
we just go back to
version x again.

00:26:41.731 --> 00:26:43.480
And everything here,
being described here,

00:26:43.480 --> 00:26:45.295
is also integrated into
Configuration Manager that's

00:26:45.295 --> 00:26:46.790
what we talked about earlier.

00:26:46.790 --> 00:26:48.690
So all these components
get orchestration

00:26:48.690 --> 00:26:50.920
together to make
sure the whole data

00:26:50.920 --> 00:26:54.550
plane works from end to end.

00:26:54.550 --> 00:26:58.480
So how does it work to have
this one global address that

00:26:58.480 --> 00:27:01.000
is usable from anywhere?

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:04.000
Mostly people talk of this
as an anycast address.

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:07.600
The large feature we have down
here is to make it stabilized,

00:27:07.600 --> 00:27:11.770
so that if things change and
routing changes over time,

00:27:11.770 --> 00:27:14.311
that doesn't have an impact.

00:27:14.311 --> 00:27:16.310
The way this works
technically is pretty simple.

00:27:16.310 --> 00:27:20.620
We announce a block of addresses
to all our peers for the UP.

00:27:20.620 --> 00:27:23.110
For each ISP, they
get to figure out

00:27:23.110 --> 00:27:26.590
what routes do they have for
this block to reach Google.

00:27:26.590 --> 00:27:29.410
And oftentimes it's as a
long list of different ones.

00:27:29.410 --> 00:27:31.930
They get to choose,
and typically they

00:27:31.930 --> 00:27:36.250
choose the shortest path, which
is the smallest number of hops.

00:27:36.250 --> 00:27:38.050
But they also have a
number of other paths

00:27:38.050 --> 00:27:41.530
they can use if that one isn't
available for some reason.

00:27:41.530 --> 00:27:44.350
So in general,
your user's traffic

00:27:44.350 --> 00:27:48.770
ends up at the narrowest POP
by network distance, which

00:27:48.770 --> 00:27:51.790
should be not that far away.

00:27:51.790 --> 00:27:55.540
And then there's a fallback
if that POP isn't available,

00:27:55.540 --> 00:27:56.680
or it goes somewhere else.

00:27:59.800 --> 00:28:02.380
Routing flaps, which
means that routing changes

00:28:02.380 --> 00:28:04.240
over short periods of
time and goes back,

00:28:04.240 --> 00:28:07.360
is fairly common, which
makes it hard to deal

00:28:07.360 --> 00:28:09.617
with these anycast addresses
for TCP and URL general.

00:28:09.617 --> 00:28:11.200
So we have a special
mechanism that we

00:28:11.200 --> 00:28:13.449
have built to deal with that,
I will talk about later.

00:28:16.410 --> 00:28:19.130
So these are the things
that we put in a POP.

00:28:19.130 --> 00:28:21.550
We have two routers
for redundancy,

00:28:21.550 --> 00:28:24.920
that are the connection
to the other networks.

00:28:24.920 --> 00:28:27.060
And then we have two more steps.

00:28:27.060 --> 00:28:31.250
First we have a network
level load balancer

00:28:31.250 --> 00:28:33.590
that does routing
on TCP connections.

00:28:33.590 --> 00:28:34.640
We call it Maglev.

00:28:34.640 --> 00:28:37.570
This is something
we built in house.

00:28:37.570 --> 00:28:41.350
So the function of that is to
map each incoming user's TCP

00:28:41.350 --> 00:28:44.500
connection to one of
the next of the boxes.

00:28:44.500 --> 00:28:47.710
The GFE, or Google
front-end for short,

00:28:47.710 --> 00:28:51.550
is the HTTP base
load that we built.

00:28:51.550 --> 00:28:54.520
So that is the first
level that can actually

00:28:54.520 --> 00:28:59.290
take the URL apart and act
based on the full constant URL.

00:28:59.290 --> 00:29:01.570
We install CDN in
these publications

00:29:01.570 --> 00:29:03.910
as well so that
for cache content,

00:29:03.910 --> 00:29:06.040
we can serve it
very quickly back

00:29:06.040 --> 00:29:10.420
and we don't even need to
send it to a data center.

00:29:10.420 --> 00:29:12.410
So let's talk more
about the Maglev.

00:29:12.410 --> 00:29:15.410
The Maglev is basically
taking five tuple hash

00:29:15.410 --> 00:29:18.470
and mapping that to
one instance of GFE.

00:29:18.470 --> 00:29:20.324
So that means that we
keep that connection

00:29:20.324 --> 00:29:22.490
at the same instance, we
have all the cache content,

00:29:22.490 --> 00:29:26.740
all the state and that
point as long as we can.

00:29:26.740 --> 00:29:31.700
So the goal is to each user,
and then a load balance fashion

00:29:31.700 --> 00:29:34.730
hits one particular GFE.

00:29:34.730 --> 00:29:37.250
And there's multiple
mechanisms that

00:29:37.250 --> 00:29:39.727
go into making that happen.

00:29:39.727 --> 00:29:41.310
Once we have established
a connection,

00:29:41.310 --> 00:29:43.580
we keep track of where we
sent that particular user

00:29:43.580 --> 00:29:45.620
and we'll keep sending
it there to make sure

00:29:45.620 --> 00:29:47.690
that it's a stable connection.

00:29:47.690 --> 00:29:50.330
If it's a new connection, so
we haven't seen it before,

00:29:50.330 --> 00:29:52.310
then we use consistent
hashing to make

00:29:52.310 --> 00:29:54.590
sure we have a
uniform way of making

00:29:54.590 --> 00:29:59.510
that decision across the board,
across this ever changing set

00:29:59.510 --> 00:30:03.000
of load balancers.

00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:07.070
To deal with these route
flaps as I mentioned before,

00:30:07.070 --> 00:30:10.770
we have a map that says
for each client prefix,

00:30:10.770 --> 00:30:12.860
where do you expect
this to land?

00:30:12.860 --> 00:30:15.180
And if a Maglev
gets traffic that

00:30:15.180 --> 00:30:16.730
isn't supposed to
land there, we'll

00:30:16.730 --> 00:30:18.560
forward it to the other Maglev.

00:30:18.560 --> 00:30:21.790
And once that route flap
isn't there anymore,

00:30:21.790 --> 00:30:23.450
the user won't see
any difference,

00:30:23.450 --> 00:30:26.600
but the other Maglev will
be doing the work now.

00:30:26.600 --> 00:30:28.770
Maglevs are only on
the inbound path,

00:30:28.770 --> 00:30:31.070
so all traffic goes
back to the user,

00:30:31.070 --> 00:30:34.460
goes out directly, which
makes it more efficient,

00:30:34.460 --> 00:30:38.330
they only have to deal
with one side of the flow.

00:30:38.330 --> 00:30:42.020
And if you do network level
load balancing for your cloud

00:30:42.020 --> 00:30:45.800
features, the Maglev will be
the ones sending direct traffic

00:30:45.800 --> 00:30:48.380
jacket to your VMs.

00:30:48.380 --> 00:30:50.570
And they get to know
what IPs to deal

00:30:50.570 --> 00:30:52.550
with from this
configuration manager

00:30:52.550 --> 00:30:54.670
that we talked about before.

00:30:54.670 --> 00:30:58.700
So these also offer
routes for kernel bypass

00:30:58.700 --> 00:31:03.880
on normal servers, and they
can do this at line rate.

00:31:03.880 --> 00:31:06.270
So this is the flow
for each Maglev.

00:31:06.270 --> 00:31:09.810
For each incoming packet, it
looks at the target IP address

00:31:09.810 --> 00:31:12.120
and says is this an
address I'm dealing with,

00:31:12.120 --> 00:31:13.890
am I doing load balance
for this address?

00:31:13.890 --> 00:31:16.010
If not, we can just drop it.

00:31:16.010 --> 00:31:20.680
If it is, we look it up in this
map that says for this prefix,

00:31:20.680 --> 00:31:21.930
it should be in this location.

00:31:21.930 --> 00:31:24.559
And if that location
is not me, well I

00:31:24.559 --> 00:31:26.850
know where to send it and I
must pass it on to that one

00:31:26.850 --> 00:31:30.030
and that one will do
the right thing with it.

00:31:30.030 --> 00:31:32.880
And this-- we
rebuild this client

00:31:32.880 --> 00:31:36.630
IP map so that we know
where to expect traffic.

00:31:36.630 --> 00:31:39.990
And then we look it up in the
connection tracking, and says,

00:31:39.990 --> 00:31:42.600
have we seen traffic
from this client before?

00:31:42.600 --> 00:31:44.130
If so, we know where to send it.

00:31:44.130 --> 00:31:46.676
If not we use consistent
hashing to figure out

00:31:46.676 --> 00:31:49.050
where it should go and we
annotate that in the connection

00:31:49.050 --> 00:31:52.384
tracking, so subsequent
packets on that TCP connection

00:31:52.384 --> 00:31:53.300
can go there directly.

00:31:56.640 --> 00:32:00.340
The next step in the
Edge POP is the GFE.

00:32:00.340 --> 00:32:03.510
It's a proxy or a reverse
proxy, so it terminates

00:32:03.510 --> 00:32:07.260
TCP connection, which
as I said before helps

00:32:07.260 --> 00:32:09.450
with making a short
connection to the user

00:32:09.450 --> 00:32:14.120
and improving latency and
improving recovery time if we--

00:32:14.120 --> 00:32:16.380
I think I dropped
packets on that link.

00:32:16.380 --> 00:32:18.870
A terminating HTTP,
and by HTTP, I

00:32:18.870 --> 00:32:22.770
mean the whole family of HTTP,
like protocols, so 1.1, 2.0.

00:32:22.770 --> 00:32:26.340
GRPC is a Google RPC
system, open source,

00:32:26.340 --> 00:32:28.290
built on top of HTTP/2.0.

00:32:28.290 --> 00:32:31.320
And QUIC is basically
the next version of HTTP,

00:32:31.320 --> 00:32:34.250
running on UDP instead of TCP.

00:32:34.250 --> 00:32:37.010
Once it actually has the
full hostname in the URL,

00:32:37.010 --> 00:32:39.249
it knows what is cacheable
and what's not cacheable,

00:32:39.249 --> 00:32:40.790
and it can consult
the cache and see,

00:32:40.790 --> 00:32:42.620
do I have this object
cache, and if so,

00:32:42.620 --> 00:32:44.730
I can flow it from
there directly.

00:32:44.730 --> 00:32:47.450
You can also do different things
depending on the full URL.

00:32:47.450 --> 00:32:50.300
So you can have different
mappings for different parts

00:32:50.300 --> 00:32:52.280
of the URL space.

00:32:52.280 --> 00:32:55.280
By default it will send
traffic to the nearest region,

00:32:55.280 --> 00:32:56.790
again to reduce latency.

00:32:56.790 --> 00:33:00.020
If that region is at
capacity and can't handle it,

00:33:00.020 --> 00:33:01.980
we'll send it somewhere else.

00:33:01.980 --> 00:33:04.570
And that capacity
can either be, you

00:33:04.570 --> 00:33:08.820
configure each VM saying this is
how much traffic it can handle,

00:33:08.820 --> 00:33:10.390
or we can just look
at the CPU list

00:33:10.390 --> 00:33:12.140
and say oh, well these
VMs are overloaded,

00:33:12.140 --> 00:33:14.950
we should not send
them more traffic.

00:33:14.950 --> 00:33:18.847
And to reduce latency, we also
health check VMs ahead of time

00:33:18.847 --> 00:33:20.430
before we send them
any traffic, so we

00:33:20.430 --> 00:33:23.349
know that they're up and
healthy, so that those requests

00:33:23.349 --> 00:33:24.140
don't have to wait.

00:33:26.790 --> 00:33:28.230
So here's an example.

00:33:28.230 --> 00:33:29.670
Assuming you have
a website where

00:33:29.670 --> 00:33:34.005
you have your assets, your
images, your HTML, your CSS,

00:33:34.005 --> 00:33:35.730
your JavaScript.

00:33:35.730 --> 00:33:37.770
Instead of serving
them from a web server,

00:33:37.770 --> 00:33:40.140
you can just put them
in a storage bucket

00:33:40.140 --> 00:33:43.040
and tell us when to put
up your load balancer.

00:33:43.040 --> 00:33:45.750
Everything that starts with
static, please go and fetch it,

00:33:45.750 --> 00:33:47.900
it's in this storage bucket.

00:33:47.900 --> 00:33:51.724
And that means that we don't
even need to go to your VM

00:33:51.724 --> 00:33:52.890
at all about these requests.

00:33:52.890 --> 00:33:55.960
We can send them
directly to the storage,

00:33:55.960 --> 00:34:00.120
which is a scaleable solution
that grows by itself.

00:34:00.120 --> 00:34:01.950
And you can have
different portions

00:34:01.950 --> 00:34:05.400
of the URL space can also get
sent to different sets of VMs.

00:34:05.400 --> 00:34:08.942
So if you have some level
of different content

00:34:08.942 --> 00:34:10.650
for different processing,
you can send it

00:34:10.650 --> 00:34:11.483
to different places.

00:34:14.469 --> 00:34:16.642
So this is the overall picture.

00:34:16.642 --> 00:34:20.560
The GFE over on the edge,
after having consulted a CDN

00:34:20.560 --> 00:34:22.707
and knows it's not
a cached content,

00:34:22.707 --> 00:34:24.790
it consults the global
load balancer on the bottom

00:34:24.790 --> 00:34:28.639
there, which will tell it, well
we have capacity in this zone,

00:34:28.639 --> 00:34:31.420
or we have capacity
in this zone.

00:34:31.420 --> 00:34:35.219
And a GFE also tells the
global load controller

00:34:35.219 --> 00:34:38.130
how much traffic it has sent, so
they can update its accounting

00:34:38.130 --> 00:34:40.739
and know not to send
too much traffic.

00:34:40.739 --> 00:34:43.939
In each zone, inside
a zone, there's

00:34:43.939 --> 00:34:45.480
a health checker
that goes and checks

00:34:45.480 --> 00:34:49.139
the VMs that are registered to
be backend for load balancer

00:34:49.139 --> 00:34:51.750
to make sure that
they are healthy.

00:34:51.750 --> 00:34:54.330
And they'd report that back
to global load controller

00:34:54.330 --> 00:34:57.750
so we wouldn't send
traffic to a VM that is not

00:34:57.750 --> 00:34:59.250
up yet or not running healthy.

00:35:02.740 --> 00:35:06.910
From a networking point of
view, the GFE looks to your VMs

00:35:06.910 --> 00:35:09.700
like a normal machine
on the network.

00:35:09.700 --> 00:35:13.420
It is using a public IP
address that's not routable,

00:35:13.420 --> 00:35:16.570
and you can think of
it as GFEs actually

00:35:16.570 --> 00:35:20.030
forming part of all your VMIDS.

00:35:20.030 --> 00:35:22.820
But GFE can tell apart
one from the other.

00:35:22.820 --> 00:35:25.720
So from your VMs and
your web servers running

00:35:25.720 --> 00:35:27.640
on your VMs point
of view, it just

00:35:27.640 --> 00:35:32.080
looks like a normal host on that
network is sending it traffic.

00:35:32.080 --> 00:35:34.052
And this is all using
the virtual networking

00:35:34.052 --> 00:35:36.010
in from Andromeda that
we earlier talked about.

00:35:38.810 --> 00:35:42.920
So to tie this back, what we
really care about and would

00:35:42.920 --> 00:35:46.440
like you to enjoy, and
your users enjoy well

00:35:46.440 --> 00:35:49.720
is low latency, and
we have built this

00:35:49.720 --> 00:35:51.241
in multiple different ways.

00:35:51.241 --> 00:35:52.990
First of all, we have
an extensive network

00:35:52.990 --> 00:35:56.755
of pairing points so that we end
up being close to your users.

00:36:00.220 --> 00:36:02.770
If we can't sort from CDN
and we have to go back to VM,

00:36:02.770 --> 00:36:06.250
we'll go to the nearest region,
again to reduce latency.

00:36:06.250 --> 00:36:08.230
We make sure everything
is warmed up,

00:36:08.230 --> 00:36:10.120
so all you have
on the connection,

00:36:10.120 --> 00:36:12.850
we are ready to send it on,
we have done the handshakes

00:36:12.850 --> 00:36:15.970
and we know that that VM
is healthy and serving

00:36:15.970 --> 00:36:18.140
and we can send it to request.

00:36:18.140 --> 00:36:21.690
And we also have done a bunch
of work on different protocols.

00:36:21.690 --> 00:36:23.810
The list of HTTP
family, particulars I

00:36:23.810 --> 00:36:26.960
listed earlier, that are all
aimed at reducing latency.

00:36:30.510 --> 00:36:33.270
As an example here,
we did a blow test

00:36:33.270 --> 00:36:36.600
across three
different zones where

00:36:36.600 --> 00:36:40.740
we pushed up the load test
to 1.3 million requests

00:36:40.740 --> 00:36:42.460
per second.

00:36:42.460 --> 00:36:46.010
And to me the present thing is
not that line, which is great,

00:36:46.010 --> 00:36:48.630
but you can see how quickly we
were able to get to that line,

00:36:48.630 --> 00:36:49.500
right.

00:36:49.500 --> 00:36:55.390
We were able to scale
things up very quickly.

00:36:55.390 --> 00:36:58.600
Now next up, Amit
is back and he's

00:36:58.600 --> 00:37:00.820
going to talk to you
how we have built

00:37:00.820 --> 00:37:03.760
all kinds of different
networking products

00:37:03.760 --> 00:37:05.571
based on this infrastructure.

00:37:12.936 --> 00:37:14.060
AMIT SINGHAL: Thanks Assar.

00:37:14.060 --> 00:37:16.400
Let's look inside
the software based

00:37:16.400 --> 00:37:19.610
design of some our
networking parts now.

00:37:19.610 --> 00:37:25.060
First part, I'll talk about
this cloud router and VPN.

00:37:25.060 --> 00:37:28.630
BGP and IPsec VPN is
often used by enterprises

00:37:28.630 --> 00:37:31.690
to provide multisite
connectivity.

00:37:31.690 --> 00:37:35.800
One of these sites can also
be your cloud provider.

00:37:35.800 --> 00:37:39.155
In a typical
deployment, VPN and BGP

00:37:39.155 --> 00:37:42.320
are in the same hardware box.

00:37:42.320 --> 00:37:44.660
BGP routing protocol
automatically

00:37:44.660 --> 00:37:46.490
learns about the
changing topology

00:37:46.490 --> 00:37:50.010
on the remote site and
programs the hardware box

00:37:50.010 --> 00:37:53.630
with forwarding decisions
for outgoing traffic.

00:37:53.630 --> 00:37:56.310
And all servers, or
VMs, send traffic

00:37:56.310 --> 00:38:01.980
to this box, which then encrypts
and sends it the other side.

00:38:01.980 --> 00:38:05.800
In GCP, there is no
hardware box involved.

00:38:05.800 --> 00:38:08.590
And BGP and VPN
running separate tasks

00:38:08.590 --> 00:38:13.060
providing control and
data plane separation.

00:38:13.060 --> 00:38:16.030
Cloud router tasks
that runs BGP programs

00:38:16.030 --> 00:38:20.950
and learns routes from the
remote peer directly on VMs.

00:38:20.950 --> 00:38:23.910
And tell VMs to send
traffic directly

00:38:23.910 --> 00:38:27.530
to the right VPN task for
those destinations, which then

00:38:27.530 --> 00:38:31.050
sends it to the other side.

00:38:31.050 --> 00:38:34.580
Our implementation provides
isolated and customizable

00:38:34.580 --> 00:38:38.210
routing and VPN functionality
for each customer,

00:38:38.210 --> 00:38:41.840
running inside their
own virtualized network.

00:38:41.840 --> 00:38:44.600
We maintain a pool of
free router and VPN

00:38:44.600 --> 00:38:47.450
tasks, which are
ready to be allocated

00:38:47.450 --> 00:38:51.962
to any virtual network
when the request comes.

00:38:51.962 --> 00:38:56.160
When Cloud VPN request comes, it
is forwarded by config manager

00:38:56.160 --> 00:38:58.750
to the VPN manager,
which then allocates

00:38:58.750 --> 00:39:01.380
a free task for this VPN.

00:39:01.380 --> 00:39:03.870
Information about
this allocated task

00:39:03.870 --> 00:39:07.710
is also sent to Fabric Manager,
which then further programs

00:39:07.710 --> 00:39:11.000
other modules downstream.

00:39:11.000 --> 00:39:13.380
Now when the cloud router
creation request comes,

00:39:13.380 --> 00:39:16.110
it is forwarded to a
routing manager, which again

00:39:16.110 --> 00:39:18.840
allocates a free routing task.

00:39:18.840 --> 00:39:21.090
And the information is
sent to Fabric Manager

00:39:21.090 --> 00:39:24.090
for programming the data plane.

00:39:24.090 --> 00:39:26.970
At the end of this, there's
a direct connectivity

00:39:26.970 --> 00:39:30.150
between your VM's
router and VPN as part

00:39:30.150 --> 00:39:33.210
of your virtual network.

00:39:33.210 --> 00:39:37.710
Now let's look at what happens
in case there's a failure.

00:39:37.710 --> 00:39:41.110
If for any reason
router task fails,

00:39:41.110 --> 00:39:45.030
routing manager identifies
that and allocates

00:39:45.030 --> 00:39:51.940
one more from the free pool, and
also updates Andromeda flows.

00:39:51.940 --> 00:39:54.580
Routing manager and data path
also preserves the routes

00:39:54.580 --> 00:39:56.960
for GBC the whole time, so
traffic is not impacted.

00:40:01.300 --> 00:40:02.875
Internet load balancing.

00:40:05.850 --> 00:40:09.600
This is a typical multi-tier
deployment for web services.

00:40:09.600 --> 00:40:14.320
Their front end web server, for
solving UI and static content,

00:40:14.320 --> 00:40:16.000
backend application
in business layer

00:40:16.000 --> 00:40:18.640
for dynamic content processing.

00:40:18.640 --> 00:40:22.210
And both these layers need
to scale out with demand.

00:40:22.210 --> 00:40:24.970
You can't forget external
load balancing of a public IP

00:40:24.970 --> 00:40:31.120
to distribute request
your front-end instances.

00:40:31.120 --> 00:40:33.850
And front-end will
further need to distribute

00:40:33.850 --> 00:40:35.830
these requests for
dynamic processing

00:40:35.830 --> 00:40:38.350
to one of the backend servers.

00:40:38.350 --> 00:40:41.200
And we want to do this
in a load balance way.

00:40:41.200 --> 00:40:43.990
And for this, we provide
manage internal load

00:40:43.990 --> 00:40:47.720
balancing our private IPs.

00:40:47.720 --> 00:40:50.740
Here we compare how our
implementation differs

00:40:50.740 --> 00:40:54.040
from a typical load balancer.

00:40:54.040 --> 00:40:56.740
Not only there is a
hardware box or task

00:40:56.740 --> 00:41:00.520
to which our traffic is sent
which then load balances

00:41:00.520 --> 00:41:03.150
between available backends.

00:41:03.150 --> 00:41:06.780
Now drawbacks for this approach
are single point of failure,

00:41:06.780 --> 00:41:09.130
and load balancer itself
can become chokepoint

00:41:09.130 --> 00:41:11.530
for handling all traffic.

00:41:11.530 --> 00:41:14.650
Therefore we got rid
of the middle box.

00:41:14.650 --> 00:41:19.120
Each client, using three or five
tuple consistent hashing picks

00:41:19.120 --> 00:41:22.060
a backend for each connection.

00:41:22.060 --> 00:41:25.120
Load balances provision
inside each client

00:41:25.120 --> 00:41:26.860
as a customizable
network function.

00:41:30.220 --> 00:41:33.650
Internal load balancer is fully
programmable through the API.

00:41:33.650 --> 00:41:36.620
Let's look at the
configuration flow.

00:41:36.620 --> 00:41:40.880
In addition to components for
conservation and control plane

00:41:40.880 --> 00:41:45.840
we have seen so far, this
health checking infrastructure.

00:41:45.840 --> 00:41:49.040
When you configure load
balancer with list of backends,

00:41:49.040 --> 00:41:53.100
health checker is asked
to monitor their health.

00:41:53.100 --> 00:41:57.220
Objects are sent to VM
controller, and based on those,

00:41:57.220 --> 00:42:00.390
it programs client VMs with
the right load balancing

00:42:00.390 --> 00:42:02.410
configuration.

00:42:02.410 --> 00:42:05.080
Client can then send traffic
directly to the backends.

00:42:10.750 --> 00:42:12.705
Now I'll talk about
Network Peering.

00:42:12.705 --> 00:42:16.180
This is a new feature which
was recently released in alpha

00:42:16.180 --> 00:42:18.310
and will be done soon.

00:42:18.310 --> 00:42:21.720
It allows you to
peer at two networks

00:42:21.720 --> 00:42:23.234
so that VMs in
those networks can

00:42:23.234 --> 00:42:24.400
communicate with each other.

00:42:27.250 --> 00:42:31.960
Common use would be software as
a service for our GCP platform.

00:42:31.960 --> 00:42:34.210
In this example,
say Google wants

00:42:34.210 --> 00:42:38.005
to provide a cloud service
to its consumers, consumer

00:42:38.005 --> 00:42:40.720
1 and consumer 2.

00:42:40.720 --> 00:42:43.560
For reasons of isolation,
building model, et cetera.

00:42:43.560 --> 00:42:46.120
It is preferred to spin
up a separate network,

00:42:46.120 --> 00:42:50.880
hosting service instances
for each customer.

00:42:50.880 --> 00:42:53.640
And then peer that network
with the corresponding consumer

00:42:53.640 --> 00:42:55.380
network.

00:42:55.380 --> 00:42:57.960
At this point, each
customer can access

00:42:57.960 --> 00:43:03.120
the service Google provided
for them across a peer link.

00:43:03.120 --> 00:43:06.160
Peering works for networks
within the same organization

00:43:06.160 --> 00:43:08.650
as well as across organization.

00:43:08.650 --> 00:43:11.980
Only restriction is that
subnetwork ranges in peering

00:43:11.980 --> 00:43:13.560
networks should not overlap.

00:43:19.640 --> 00:43:23.640
Peering implementation provides
isolation with connectivity.

00:43:23.640 --> 00:43:27.050
We talked about VM
controller earlier.

00:43:27.050 --> 00:43:29.720
There is an Endpoint
Manager game controller

00:43:29.720 --> 00:43:34.220
that handles all the updates
given to it by Fabric Manager.

00:43:34.220 --> 00:43:37.190
It gives endpoints for each
network, the per network flow

00:43:37.190 --> 00:43:41.450
handler that creates all
the flows for the network.

00:43:41.450 --> 00:43:45.020
Now for peering, we make
endpoint managers smarter

00:43:45.020 --> 00:43:48.020
to understand peering
confrontation.

00:43:48.020 --> 00:43:51.620
Which make network
handlers aware of endpoints

00:43:51.620 --> 00:43:54.500
in peer networks also.

00:43:54.500 --> 00:43:57.530
These network handlers
then also cleared floors

00:43:57.530 --> 00:44:02.030
for sending traffic to
endpoints in peer network.

00:44:02.030 --> 00:44:05.480
But each network can still
control what kind of traffic

00:44:05.480 --> 00:44:08.540
they can receive,
from peer network

00:44:08.540 --> 00:44:12.010
through ingress firewall rules.

00:44:12.010 --> 00:44:15.370
So this how we provide
isolation with connectivity.

00:44:15.370 --> 00:44:17.990
Peering was implemented
as network function

00:44:17.990 --> 00:44:20.555
with big feature turn
around, by modifications

00:44:20.555 --> 00:44:23.580
in only the centralized control
plane in a true SDN way.

00:44:27.300 --> 00:44:30.690
There are many more networking
features in GCP like multi NIC,

00:44:30.690 --> 00:44:33.270
IP addressing, firewalls,
that we could not

00:44:33.270 --> 00:44:35.220
cover here due to time.

00:44:35.220 --> 00:44:38.340
But before closing, I
want to introduce Google's

00:44:38.340 --> 00:44:41.830
virtual private cloud, VPC.

00:44:41.830 --> 00:44:45.210
Google provides unique
VPC characteristics.

00:44:45.210 --> 00:44:49.860
It provides global connectivity
for VMs in multiple regions,

00:44:49.860 --> 00:44:53.520
so that they can talk to
each other or private IPs.

00:44:53.520 --> 00:44:57.300
It is shareable across
multiple projects

00:44:57.300 --> 00:45:01.750
and some Network ranges can
be extended with demand.

00:45:01.750 --> 00:45:04.360
It also provides access
to Google services

00:45:04.360 --> 00:45:07.610
without traversing
public network.

00:45:07.610 --> 00:45:10.696
There is also full talk later
in the evening on this topic.

00:45:14.350 --> 00:45:16.330
To summarize, what
are the benefits

00:45:16.330 --> 00:45:18.890
of Google Cloud platform.

00:45:18.890 --> 00:45:21.250
It provides on demand
access to compute, storage,

00:45:21.250 --> 00:45:23.230
and networking.

00:45:23.230 --> 00:45:29.140
High availability and uptimes,
high throughput, low latency,

00:45:29.140 --> 00:45:32.080
network functions
as a service, it

00:45:32.080 --> 00:45:35.530
has been using same
infrastructure and innovations

00:45:35.530 --> 00:45:39.420
that powered Google services,
and will deliver products

00:45:39.420 --> 00:45:42.730
at the speed of software.

00:45:42.730 --> 00:45:44.810
To learn more, these
are some other talks

00:45:44.810 --> 00:45:49.200
which will be happening
this evening or tomorrow.

